WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:10.520
Now the lift function can go and extract specific number of characters from the start of a string value.

00:10.520 --> 00:15.240
So if you want to get few characters at the beginning of a value, you can use the lift.

00:15.240 --> 00:17.840
But now the write function is exactly the opposite.

00:17.880 --> 00:22.960
It can go and extract specific number of characters from the end of string value.

00:22.960 --> 00:27.160
So if you want few characters from the end of your value, you can use right now.

00:27.160 --> 00:32.000
In order to apply the lift or the right function, you have to give SQL two things the value where you

00:32.000 --> 00:36.960
want to extract apart from it, and the number of characters, how many characters you want to extract.

00:36.960 --> 00:38.960
And this is the same for the left and the right.

00:39.000 --> 00:41.240
Now let's say that we have again this value, Mariam.

00:41.240 --> 00:45.120
And now the task says, I would like to extract the first two characters.

00:45.120 --> 00:49.280
And since we are talking about the starting position, we're going to use the lift function.

00:49.280 --> 00:51.840
And since it says two characters, we're going to go with the two.

00:51.880 --> 00:57.200
So it's going to start counting M is one, A is two, and after that it's going to stop and make a cut,

00:57.240 --> 00:59.520
and it's going to go and return the two characters.

00:59.560 --> 01:00.040
Ma.

01:00.160 --> 01:04.130
So we are counting from the left side going to the right side right now.

01:04.130 --> 01:10.290
If your task says extract the last two characters, here we are talking about the end position of your

01:10.330 --> 01:15.090
value, and for that we're going to use the right function since we are approaching from the right side,

01:15.090 --> 01:18.330
and since we want only two characters, the number of characters is going to be two.

01:18.370 --> 01:22.450
So this time it's going to start counting from the right side, moving to the left side.

01:22.450 --> 01:25.450
So A is one, I is two and that's it.

01:25.450 --> 01:30.730
Then a square is going to stop and extract only those two characters I so if you want to extract data

01:30.770 --> 01:32.810
at the starting position you use the left.

01:32.810 --> 01:38.370
But if you want to extract characters from the end position of your value, then you use the right function.

01:38.370 --> 01:40.770
Now let's go back to scalar order to practice okay.

01:40.810 --> 01:42.130
So now we have the following task.

01:42.130 --> 01:45.290
Retrieve the first two characters of each first name.

01:45.290 --> 01:47.370
So we just need the first two characters.

01:47.410 --> 01:51.690
Since we are coming from the left side, we can go and use the function lift.

01:51.970 --> 01:53.930
So it's very simple first name.

01:54.250 --> 01:56.090
And we need only two characters.

01:56.090 --> 01:56.570
So two.

01:56.610 --> 01:59.730
So we're going to call it first two character.

01:59.770 --> 02:01.050
Let's go and execute it.

02:01.050 --> 02:03.930
And now you can see in the output we have two characters Ma.

02:04.050 --> 02:07.910
Now with John we have only G because we have a the leading space.

02:07.910 --> 02:10.590
Well, you can leave it like this or you can transform it.

02:10.590 --> 02:12.390
And then George, we have GE and so on.

02:12.390 --> 02:16.150
So with that, we are getting the first two characters now in order to fix it for Joan, what we're

02:16.150 --> 02:20.670
going to do, we're going to say trim first and then apply the lift.

02:20.670 --> 02:24.670
So with that we are getting rid of all white spaces and then we apply the lift.

02:24.670 --> 02:26.630
So with that everything looks perfect.

02:26.630 --> 02:28.110
So for John we have Geo.

02:28.110 --> 02:31.590
So this is how we can get the first two characters of a column.

02:31.590 --> 02:32.870
Now let's move to the next one.

02:32.870 --> 02:36.950
The task says retrieve the last two characters of each first name.

02:36.950 --> 02:38.750
So this time we need the last two.

02:38.790 --> 02:41.910
So we are coming from the right side so we can do it like this.

02:41.910 --> 02:49.510
We're going to say right first name and then as well to the last two character.

02:49.550 --> 02:50.910
Let's go and execute it.

02:50.950 --> 02:55.030
And now as you can see in the output we have new column where we have the last two characters from the

02:55.030 --> 02:55.670
first name.

02:55.670 --> 02:59.710
So we have here IA air and for John as well working.

